    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Each grade can now have its own terms, and a claim's due-by is fixed when the claim is created. `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**Stored data**\n- New migration `V23__claim_due_by.sql` adds a `due_by` column to `claim`. Claims that already exist get their date plus the sheet's terms (14 if the sheet never set any). No per-grade terms existed before this change, so that is the due-by they would have had.\n\n**Server**\n- `setting/Terms.java` now works out a grade's terms: the grade's own `terms-<grade>` setting if it has one, otherwise the sheet's `terms`, otherwise 14.\n- `CreateClaim` sets the due-by when the claim is made. It uses the terms of the person's grade on the claim's date, so a dated grade change counts. Saving or editing a claim never touches `due_by` again, so later changes to the terms or to the claim's date leave it alone.\n- `terms-A`, `terms-B` and `terms-C` are accepted as settings: blank (which clears it) or a whole number of days. Changing one writes the usual `SETTING_CHANGED key=terms-<grade> value=<days>` line, and the settings list returns them, blank until set.\n- The late charge, and so the rollups, totals and statements, now uses the stored due-by instead of working it out from the current terms.\n- Each claim returned by the server now includes its `dueBy`.\n\n**Front end**\n- The settings form has Terms A, Terms B and Terms C fields (`setting-terms-<grade>` and `setting-terms-<grade>-current`).\n- The screen shows the claim's stored due-by and decides overdue from it; it no longer works it out from the current terms.\n\nA few behaviours to be aware of:\n- **Moving a claim to another person keeps its due-by**, even if that person is on a grade with different terms. That follows \"fixed when the claim is created\", but the request doesn't cover it directly.\n- **Changing terms no longer changes any figure**, so a terms change writes no `ROLLUP`/`TOTALS` lines. I updated the comment in the settings endpoint to match.\n- **\"Terms in force on the claim's date\"**: terms have no from-dates, so this means the terms set when the claim is created, applied to the grade the person has on the claim's date.",
